window.docContents[105].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--Periodicity-and-zeros":{"contents":"Source: DLMF §4.28, Periodicity and Zeros.\n\nThe hyperbolic sine and cosine have period 2πi, while hyperbolic tangent has\nperiod πi.  Their zeros are\nsinh z = 0 at z = i k π and cosh z = 0 at\nz = i(k+1/2)π, for integers k.  The single Lean declaration records both\nperiods and both zero families in one reusable proposition.\n\n\n\nThe source notes that the hyperbolic identities inherit many properties from\nthe corresponding trigonometric functions.  For background references, it\npoints to Hobson (1928), pp. 322–326, and Levinson and Redheffer (1970),\npp. 56–57.\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.28 Definitions and Periodicity\u0009§4.28 Definitions and Periodicity","header":"Periodicity and zeros","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--Periodicity-and-zeros"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___34-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___34-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___34-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations":{"contents":"All variables are complex.  E1–E6 are local derivative identities; reciprocal\nfunctions are stated only away from their poles.  E7–E10 are differential\nequation predicates for a candidate w, whereas E11–E14 record the displayed\nsolution families or their derivative consequences with a ≠ 0 and the\nrelevant pole conditions explicit.  This keeps local calculus facts distinct\nfrom global classifications.  The source points to Kamke for further ODEs.\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}\\sinh z=\\cosh z$$\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}\\cosh z=\\sinh z$$\n\nThe quotient derivatives are tanh' = sech², csch' = -csch·coth,\nsech' = -sech·tanh, and coth' = -csch².  Their Lean statements expose\nnonzero denominator hypotheses rather than silently treating poles as ordinary\npoints.  The four source links are E3,\nE4, E5, and\nE6.\n\nFor a ≠ 0, the source introduces the second-order equation\nw''-a²w=0, the two first integrals with right sides 1 and -1, and the\nRiccati equation w'+a²w²=1.  The reusable predicates below are instantiated\nonly on the named hyperbolic solution families; they do not assert an ODE for\nan arbitrary unconstrained function.  See the\nindividual source links E7,\nE8, E9, and\nE10.\n\nThe complete second-order family is A cosh(az)+B sinh(az); the remaining\nsource displays give the sinh, cosh, and coth parametrizations with arbitrary\nconstant c.  The Lean signatures retain a ≠ 0, and E14 excludes zeros of\nsinh(az+c) before differentiating the coth chart.  Direct links: E11, E12, E13, and E14.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.34 Derivatives and Differential Equations","header":"§4.34 Derivatives and Differential Equations","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___34-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___34-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___34-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations"}});